## Anti-escape rules

Nine traps to avoid:

**Rule 1 — Location ≠ Kind status.** Q3.L1's location priority
(`sitecustomize` > scaffolding > minimal src) only applies after Q1
classifies the patch as Kind 1. **Writing Kind 2 logic into
`/tmp/sitecustomize.py` does not make it a shim.** The attribution test
is tool-independent: a sitecustomize file that adds executable logic
determining a test result is Kind 2, just like the same code in src
would be. Location selection comes after Q1, never around it.

**Rule 2 — Collection-collapse guard.** When
`H_total_not_below_base.value` is near zero (collected ≪ baseline),
src is not loading at all. **That is Kind 1 (evaluation repair /
loading-layer), not Kind 2 (behavior substitution), regardless of how
many missing symbols you spot.** Write the smallest shim that restores
loading, then rerun. Do NOT file mass `unbound_issue` entries or set
`agent_sufficient=true` before collection recovers. Once collection
recovers, reclassify the remaining failures under the attribution
test: real behavior gaps are Kind 2 and should become waivers /
signals, not increasingly capable shims.

**Rule 3 — Route vs drop for collection hooks.** Adding
`pytest_collection_modifyitems` skip logic that drops tests is
forbidden (WHY). **Hooks that route, order, or organize tests without
dropping any are HOW-layer.** The same flags (`--ignore`, `-k`,
`addopts`, package excludes) are silently-dropping (WHY) when
nothing else runs the excluded tests; they are routing (HOW) when
they direct a known set into a separate `test_config.json` mode.
Document any routing in `unbound_issues.evidence`.

**Rule 4 — Captured-reference deadlock has no L1 location.** When
`from x import Y` has captured the old binding before any hook can
intercept (mechanism in Q1 Edge cases above), Q1 says Kind 1 but Q2
has no legal L1 — emit `commit_signal` or `unbound_issue`. Do not
upgrade the shim into Kind 2 just because the Kind-1 location is
unreachable. The Q1/Q2 split is the whole point: classification and
location feasibility are independent decisions.

**Rule 5 — "Working" backward-compat wrapper.** Wrappers whose body
produces a usable value (async wrapping sync, new-API method
delegating to old-API, etc.) are presumed Kind 2 because they substitute
behavior, not just evaluability. Do not write one merely because it is
short or backward-compatible. It can enter the signal-pollution
tie-breaker only when all three are true: (i) it is required solely to
prevent collection / setup collapse, (ii) no in-scope test observes the
wrapper as the behavior API being measured, and (iii) the specific
in-scope test set is verified to lose signal (`P→P` / `F→F`) without
creating fake pass signal. If the wrapper introduces fake `F→P`, or if
a test's behavioral question is answered by the wrapper, it remains
Kind 2.
- `async def aX(...): return X_sync(...)` — async wrapper delegation
- `class Middleware: def __call__(req): return get_response(req)` —
  pass-through middleware
- `def log_actions(...): for obj in queryset: self.log_action(...)` —
  bulk method delegating to per-row method
- `def simple_block_tag(...): return self.simple_tag(...)` — new
  decorator delegating to existing decorator

**Rule 5 exception — pure rename alias.** When the future commit's
ONLY effect is renaming an existing symbol (same observable contract,
no semantic shift), a plain assignment alias is shape 7 (re-export)
and is Kind 1:

```python
# Hypothetical: commit renames foo() to bar() with identical semantics
bar = foo   # Kind 1 — pure re-export, no behavior change
```

The deciding evidence is the future commit's description: a commit
titled "Rename X to Y" / "Deprecate X in favor of Y" with no other
src changes is a rename. A commit titled "Add async support for X" /
"Introduce batched Y" describes a *new feature*, not a rename. When
in doubt, treat as semantic shift (Kind 2) — a rename misread as a
new feature only loses the alias's import-resolution benefit; a new
feature misread as a rename masks the milestone signal.

Critically: **async/sync wrappers are NEVER renames.** The async
function's observability differs from the sync function on multiple
axes (returns coroutine vs value; supports `await`; can be inspected
as `inspect.iscoroutine`; raises differently in event loops). Even
if `await new_async(...)` produces the same value as `old_sync(...)`,
the type / call protocol / inspectability are observable to tests
and are different. Async wrappers stay Kind 2 under all
circumstances.

**Rule 6 — System-check no-op.** Silencing framework startup
validation (`check_* → []`, `run_checks → None`, `_check_<rule> →
[]`) returns a working value and is therefore dangerous. It is Kind 2
whenever an in-scope test asserts the check result, because the no-op
answers that test's behavioral question artificially. Treat it as a
temporary evaluation repair only when the check is acting as a suite
startup gate and no in-scope test observes the check's return value;
then it merely lets unrelated tests reach their real outcomes. In that
startup-gate case, use the tie-breaker: acceptable only when it avoids
collection collapse without creating fake `F→P`. Mark the shim as
`interim_until_split` in `module2_waivers.json` and emit an
`unbound_issue(kind="structural_span_overflow")` so M3 can address via
milestone-split.

**Rule 7 — Enum value enumeration is Kind 2 when value drives
behavior.** Defining `class ShowFacets(Enum): NEVER, ALLOW, ALWAYS`
or `class WindowFrameExclusion(IntEnum): CURRENT_ROW, GROUP, TIES,
NO_OTHERS` populates dispatch keys that downstream src branches on
(admin filter selection, SQL window-frame generation). Even if the
enum values are "just constants," they cause real test outcomes to
flip from error to pass via the branches they unlock. The correct
Kind 1 form is shape 2 (empty class) — `class ShowFacets: pass` —
which lets the import succeed but produces an enum-instanceless
class; any `ShowFacets.NEVER` access still raises `AttributeError`.

**Rule 8 — Test-list curation is silent drop (Rule 3 in disguise).**
Selecting a curated list of nodeids (`maint032_selected_tests.txt`,
`-k "test_a or test_b or test_c"`, conftest filter that admits only
named tests) when no other `test_config.json` mode covers the
excluded tests is silent drop. Even if the rationale is "keep
H_f2f_not_above_base under cap" or "exclude flaky tests" — silent
drop, no exception. Either route excluded tests into a separate mode
or accept the gate failure honestly.

**Rule 9 — Reversing test-isolation invariants is Kind 2.** Replacing
pytest-django's `DjangoDbBlocker.block` with its inverse so non-
`@django_db` tests silently get DB access, monkey-patching
`pytest_collection_modifyitems` to add markers, or otherwise
relaxing the test framework's isolation guarantees flips test
outcomes from error→pass at scale. Test infrastructure can change
HOW (workers, timeouts, paths) but not the contract that determines
WHY a test passes.

